      <p class="note"><a href="/guides.html">Guides</a> / QR codes</p>
      <h1>How QR Codes Work and How to Make a Good One</h1>
      <p class="lead">A QR code is a square grid of black and white modules that stores text, usually a web address. A phone camera reads the pattern and opens the link. The idea is simple, but a few choices decide whether a code scans reliably.</p>

      <h2>What is inside a QR code</h2>
      <p>The three large squares in the corners help a scanner find and orient the code. The smaller squares and the timing pattern help it correct for angle and distortion. The rest of the grid holds the data, plus error correction bits that let the code be read even when part of it is damaged.</p>

      <div class="ad-slot" aria-label="Advertisement"></div>

      <h2>Error correction levels</h2>
      <p>You choose one of four levels when you make a code:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>L (about 7%)</strong> gives the smallest code, but it fails quickly if the code is scratched or dirty.</li>
        <li><strong>M (about 15%)</strong> is a good default for most uses.</li>
        <li><strong>Q (about 25%)</strong> suits codes that may get worn or partly covered.</li>
        <li><strong>H (about 30%)</strong> is the most robust. Use it if you plan to put a logo in the centre.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Higher levels make the code denser, so it needs to be printed larger.</p>

      <h2>Print size and contrast</h2>
      <p>Keep the code at least about 2 cm square when printed, and scan it from the distance people will actually use. Leave a clear margin of white space around it. Use dark code on a light background. Inverted codes, such as white on black, scan poorly on many phones.</p>

      <h2>Keep links short and test them</h2>
      <p>Shorter text means a simpler code. Always scan the code with a real phone before printing it, and check the link opens the right page. A code cannot be edited after it is printed, so if the address changes, the code stops working.</p>

      <h2>Try it yourself</h2>
      <p>The <a href="../tools/qr-code-generator.html">QR Code Generator</a> lets you choose the size and error correction level, then download the code as a PNG.</p>
      <section class="worked">
        <h2>Worked example</h2>
        <p>A QR code with medium error correction (about 15%) can still scan if roughly that share of the code is damaged or covered. A code for a short link at 4 cm square prints clearly from a normal distance, while a very dense code made from a long link may need a larger print size.</p>
      </section>
      <section class="faq-list">
        <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
        <details><summary>Do QR codes expire?</summary><p>A static code does not expire. The link it points to can change or stop working, so keep your destinations up to date.</p></details>
        <details><summary>Can I put a logo in the middle?</summary><p>Yes, but use high error correction (H) and keep the logo small, so enough of the code is still readable.</p></details>
        <details><summary>Why won't my code scan?</summary><p>Common causes are a very small print size, low contrast, inverted colours or a code that is too dense for its size.</p></details>
      </section>
